    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fractures, including small faults, are believed to impact hydrocarbon flow during secondary migration, when hydrocarbons displace in-situ brine in carrier beds, after being expelled from a source. However, evidence that elucidates how fractures might enhance or impede hydrocarbon flow during secondary migration is rarely available. In this paper, we assess the potential impacts of densely fractured beds and small faults on the secondary migration of hydrocarbons by integrating outcrop observations with flow simulations. We focus on a specific case: a volume of the Anacacho Limestone exposed in an asphalt mine located in southwest Texas. By inferring that the present day asphalt distribution is evidence of paleo-hydrocarbon flow, we suggest that asphalt stains on fault surfaces and asphalt fill within fracture apertures indicate that fractures and faults once acted as focused flow conduits. To carry out this study, field maps of faults, heavily fractured beds, and stratigraphic contacts were combined with petrographic descriptions from 75 subsurface wells surrounding the mine to create a 3D geologic model. Based upon this model we performed a series of flow simulations to investigate different secondary hydrocarbon migration flow scenarios. By comparing our simulation results to known asphalt concentration trends and kriged spatial estimates of measured asphalt concentration values, we conclude that a normal fault with 4m of throw likely served as the primary conduit through which hydrocarbons entered this volume of the Anacacho Limestone. Additionally, the migration of oil within the Anacacho Limestone was strongly influenced by high degrees of permeability anisotropy, the magnitude and orientation of which appears to be consistent with the existing distribution of fractures in the formation. Thus we offer a plausible migration scenario where small faults and other fractures provide key pathways for the secondary migration of hydrocarbons.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
